Pelagic Strut

Ingredients: SOUL JAZZ / JAZZ-FUNK + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Vocoder
Creative Catalyst: The genre apes the call-and-response of container ships greeting each other in fog.

Pelagic Strut takes the greasy, pocket-heavy swing of 1970s jazz-funk and drenches it in the oppressive, moisture-heavy decay of semi-automated port cities. It sounds like a Fender Rhodes electric piano leaking hydraulic fluid while a robot choir attempts to sing Jimmy Smith solos through a broken air-horn. Deep, syncopated electric basslines pull the track forward, but the harmonic center is dominated by low-frequency vocal synthesis and long, reverberant horn patches that mimic distant oceanic freight signals answering one another across miles of brackish water.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 92–104 BPM. Heavy, drunken syncopation with a severe laid-back pocket; the hi-hats drag behind the snare like heavy iron chains dragging across wet steel decks.
- Harmonic Palette: Minor 9th chords, dominant 7#9 "Hendrix" chords, and suspended pentatonic scales, corrupted by microtonal pitch-bends that imitate the shifting pressure of marine foghorns.
- Production Signatures: Gated spring reverbs set to mimic cavernous cargo holds; extreme sidechain compression keyed to low-register vocoder carrier signals; tape-saturation plugin chains mimicking saltwater-corroded magnetic heads.
- Dynamics: Long, moody plateaus of ambient sub-bass and fog-call vocoders suddenly breaking into explosive, sweat-drenched clavinet and brass jams before sinking back into wet stillness.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the mid-2040s along the salt-crusted docks of the Rotterdam-Kiel Industrial Strait, Pelagic Strut was invented by off-shift automated-crane technicians and night-market buskers. Deprived of sunlight beneath the towering stacks of mag-lev freight containers, these musicians hooked modified Sennheiser vocoders directly into the low-frequency marine radio channels, jamming along with the algorithmic fog horns of uncrewed cargo ships clearing the harbor. The subculture—known as "Deck-Heads"—is defined by grease-stained high-vis oilskins, customized portable PA rigs strapped to re-engineered forklift chassis, and all-night dance parties held inside emptied, salt-washed shipping crates. The music acts as a visceral counter-hymn to industrial automation: human soul-jazz chops wrestling with the mechanical calls of global shipping logs.

Instruments Used
- Vocal Processing: Electro-Harmonix V256 and vintage Sennheiser VSM201 Vocoders (fed by carrier signals from low-frequency synth brass).
- Keys & Rhythm Section: Fender Rhodes Mark I (run through a saturated space echo), Hohner Clavinet D6, and heavily damp-damped 1970s Ludwig acoustic drum kits.
- Synthesizers & Bass: Minimoog Model D (for subterranean, oily basslines) and ARP Odyssey (for microtonal foghorn leads).

